    {
      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>In Scotland, a Care Inspectorate visit is not a surprise if you have already run it on yourself. Childminders are one of the largest groups the Care Inspectorate registers, and childminding in Scotland is regulated by the Care Inspectorate, not Ofsted, which covers England only, and not CQC. The Care Inspectorate expects a childminder to self-evaluate honestly and continuously against its quality framework, so the grades an inspector awards should rarely be a shock. The real question is not whether to self-evaluate, it is an expectation, but how to do it so the evaluation is honest, the evidence is captured at the point of observation, and the improvement plan stays live rather than a page written the night before a visit.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>In Scotland the Care Inspectorate regulates childminders, not Ofsted, which regulates childcare in England, and not CQC.</li><li>Childminders are measured against the Health and Social Care Standards and the Care Inspectorate's quality framework for childminding and early learning and childcare, graded on a six-point scale from unsatisfactory to excellent.</li><li>Self-evaluation is an ongoing expectation, not a one-off form: you judge your own service honestly against the quality indicators and evidence each judgement.</li><li>An honest self-evaluation finds gaps early and builds evidence; it does not raise the grade. Acting on it does.</li><li>Quickler captures your self-evaluation walk, your safety and premises checks and your evidence, on the phone you already carry. It is not a children's records, learning journal, register or child protection system.</li><li>For a sole childminder, the free tier of 20 reports a month is usually plenty. Quickler is not affiliated with or endorsed by the Care Inspectorate, and whether your evidence satisfies an inspection is a judgement for you and your inspector.</li></ul>"
    },
