      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The framework",
      "heading": "The Care Inspectorate quality framework, briefly",
      "html": "<p>The Care Inspectorate assesses a childminding service against the Health and Social Care Standards, which set out what people should expect from their care, and its quality framework for childminding and early learning and childcare, which organises the evaluation into key questions. These cover, in general terms, how good the care, play and learning are, how good your setting is, how good you are as the person providing the care, and how well you lead and improve your own service. Each key question breaks into quality indicators that describe what strong practice looks like, and services are graded on a six-point scale from unsatisfactory to excellent.</p><p>Self-evaluation mirrors this. You walk your own service against the same key questions and quality indicators, grade yourself honestly, and record the evidence for each. The aim is not to award yourself a flattering grade; it is to find the gaps an inspector would find, while there is time to close them. Always work from the current published framework and standards, as the Care Inspectorate updates its guidance, and treat this page as general information, not regulatory advice.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Working alone",
      "heading": "Record-keeping when it is just you",
      "html": "<p>A childminder wears every hat. You are the practitioner, the manager, the health and safety lead and the person who keeps the paperwork, all at once, usually while minding children. That is exactly why record-keeping slips: not because you do not know the standard, but because there is no quiet office moment to write it up. So it gets done later, from memory, and the record becomes a claim rather than evidence.</p><p>Capturing on your phone at the point of the check fixes that. The garden gate you checked, the smoke alarm you tested, the outing you risk-assessed, the sleep routine you followed: a quick message, a photo, a voice note, and the evidence exists with a date attached. When the Care Inspectorate visits, your self-evaluation is backed by a live record you built as you went, not reconstructed the night before.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The duty",
      "heading": "Keeping the improvement plan live",
      "html": "<p>Self-evaluation in the Scottish system is not a form you fill in once a year. It is an expectation that you reflect on your own practice continuously, know your own strengths and weaknesses, and drive your own improvement. An inspector will look at how well you understand your service and whether your self-evaluation is honest and matched by action. A childminder who grades generously and cannot show the improvement work behind the grade fares worse than one who is candid about a weakness and can show what they are doing about it.</p><p>You are accountable for acting on what the self-evaluation finds. A self-evaluation that surfaces a lapsed certificate, a tired resource or a routine that needs tightening is worth nothing if it never becomes a job you do. Quickler turns each finding into an action with a date, so your self-evaluation becomes an improvement plan you keep live, not a page you rewrite before each visit.</p>"
